---
.title = "Capability networking and durable state in Zig",
.description = "OCapN and CapTP networking with Loam transactional storage and durable actors. A low-level Zig library, Apache-2.0, 0.1.5 alpha.",
.date = .date("2026-06-12T00:00:00"),
.layout = "index.shtml",
.draft = false,
---

glowcap is a low-level [Zig](https://ziglang.org/) library for capability-based
communication and durable application state. It combines
[OCapN](https://github.com/ocapn/ocapn) and CapTP networking with
[Loam]($link.page('loam')), its transactional storage and durable actor system.
Applications control memory, I/O, and scheduling.

Peers pass references to objects and call them across a network. Loam keeps
the objects' state and relationships on disk, wakes actors on demand, and
provides durable names peers can use after a restart.

[Their program hands yours a reference to an object called "greeter." Holding that reference is what lets you call it.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-capability.svg'))

[Why OCapN]($link.page('why')) compares capabilities with bearer tokens and
login tables. [What about identity?]($link.page('identity')) covers where
people, bots, and AI agents fit, and what keys can and cannot answer.

**0.1.5 alpha · Apache-2.0.** APIs, ABIs, and storage formats can change before
1.0. Read [Project & status]($link.page('project')) before depending on it.

# Try it

[Rooms]($link.page('rooms')) is a chat app built on glowcap. The app is
[a page on this site]($link.siteAsset('rooms/app/index.html')). The
server runs on your machine: it hosts the directory your rooms live in, and
starting it is one command:

```sh
curl -fsSL https://glowcap.org/install.sh | sh
export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"
glowcap apps rooms serve
```

Open the link it prints: that page, with your server's address in it. Your
session stays in your browser, and your directory runs on your machine. Pick a
member in
a room and press **Message *name* directly** to talk without the room in the
middle. [Run Rooms]($link.page('rooms/start')) takes it from there, and the
[CapTP CLI]($link.page('cli')) calls and hosts capabilities from your shell,
with a REPL and MCP tools.

The install command verifies the release checksum before anything runs.
[GitHub Releases](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/releases) lists the
downloads and their provenance.

# Build with it

A Zig program dials a peer over encrypted TCP, fetches a capability it published,
and calls it:

```zig
var hint_storage: [1]glowcap.ocapn.model.HintEntry = undefined;
const peer_key = "<64 lowercase hex characters from the server>";
const partner = glowcap.net.tcp.noise.locator(
    peer_key,
    "127.0.0.1:9099",
    &hint_storage,
);
const session = try glowcap.driver.connect(
    allocator,
    io,
    .{ .symbols = &.{.noise_tcp} },
    partner,
    .ephemeral,
    .{ .handshake = .{ .ms = 10_000 }, .settle = .{ .ms = 10_000 } },
);
defer session.deinit();
const greeter = try session.fetch("greeter"); // a capability this peer published
var arena = std.heap.ArenaAllocator.init(allocator);
defer arena.deinit();
const settled = try session.call(greeter, "greet", &.{.{ .string = "world" }}, arena.allocator());
```

[Start building]($link.page('start')) shows the listening side and the same
call in other languages. Use glowcap as a Zig package, a C ABI, or a binding
for Rust, TypeScript, Python, or Racket. The TypeScript binding also runs in
the browser over WebAssembly. [Choose an API]($link.page('surfaces')) compares
the entry points.

The protocol core is a synchronous state machine with no I/O of its own, so
sockets, threads, and scheduling stay in your program. Peers can connect over
[iroh]($link.page('transports/iroh')), [Noise]($link.page('transports/noise')),
[WebRTC]($link.page('transports/webrtc')), or [Tor]($link.page('transports/onion')),
among other paths. [Choosing a transport]($link.page('transports/choosing'))
compares reachability, encryption, and identity.

[Performance]($link.page('performance')) reports measured costs and checked
allocation and copy budgets. [What you can depend on]($link.page('stability'))
explains which behavior is tested and where outside testing is still needed.

AI coding assistants help write glowcap. The maintainer directs the work
and is answerable for what lands.
[How glowcap is built](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/PROVENANCE.md)
records the process.
